pr.solution:
function hexToRgb(hex) {
  const r = parseInt(hex.substr(1, 2), 16);
  const g = parseInt(hex.substr(3, 2), 16);
  const b = parseInt(hex.substr(5, 2), 16);
  return "rgb(" + r + "," + g + "," + b + ")";
}

function winner(rules) {
  function rank(sel) {
    if (sel === "inline") return 4;
    if (sel[0] === "#") return 3;
    if (sel[0] === ".") return 2;
    return 1;
  }
  let best = null;
  for (const r of rules) {
    if (best === null || rank(r.sel) >= rank(best.sel)) best = r;
  }
  return best.value;
}

ex.q:
In the file below, which lines are PHP code and which are non-PHP code?<pre>&lt;html&gt;&lt;body&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;My page&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;?php echo "Hi"; ?&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;</pre>

cards.en:
Rules to remember when writing PHP:<ul><li>All PHP code goes between <code>&lt;?php</code> and <code>?&gt;</code>.</li><li><b>Every statement must end with a semicolon ;</b> (missing one gives a syntax error).</li><li>Non-PHP code is copied directly to the resulting HTML.</li><li>PHP has <b>no indentation or line-break requirement</b> (unlike Python); one statement can span several lines. Indent anyway for readability.</li><li>PHP code can be placed <b>anywhere</b> in the file, and there can be <b>many</b> &lt;?php ?&gt; blocks, even inside an HTML tag's content.</li></ul><pre>&lt;h1&gt;&lt;?php echo "Welcome!"; ?&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
&lt;?php
  echo "This is my homepage.";
?&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</pre>Here two PHP blocks sit inside HTML elements; the interpreter replaces each block with what it echoes.

ex.q:
Write the resulting HTML of:<pre>&lt;h2&gt;
&lt;?php echo "Hello World!"; ?&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;</pre>

What is displayed on the page?<pre>&lt;?php
echo "Good";
echo "bye";
echo "&lt;br&gt;See you";
?&gt;</pre>

Trace and give the final values:<pre>$x = 5; $y = 18;
$x++;
$z = $y / $x;
$x += $y;</pre>

cards.en:
The <b>dot operator .</b> joins strings into one string. The result can be assigned to a variable or echoed directly.<pre>$opponent = "Tiffany";
echo $opponent . ", you can't beat me.";
// Tiffany, you can't beat me.</pre>Key points:<ul><li><b>+ cannot join strings</b> in PHP (unlike Python); it tries to add numbers.</li><li>Spaces are <b>not</b> added automatically; put them inside the strings.</li><li>Use <code>.=</code> to append: <code>$s .= "!";</code></li></ul>Book Example 2.1:<pre>$phone = "1234 5678";
$email = "andyng@playstation.me";
echo "&lt;p&gt;You may contact me at " . $phone . ".&lt;br&gt;";
echo "Alternatively, " . $email . " is my email address.&lt;/p&gt;";</pre>Resulting HTML: <code>&lt;p&gt;You may contact me at 1234 5678.&lt;br&gt;Alternatively, andyng@playstation.me is my email address.&lt;/p&gt;</code>; the page shows two lines.

The welcomePage problem from 2.1 solved with PHP:<pre>Good
&lt;?php
  $time_period = date('a', time());
  if ($time_period == "am") {
    echo "morning";
  } else {
    echo "afternoon";
  }
?&gt;
, welcome to my website.</pre><ul><li><code>time()</code> returns the current time.</li><li><code>date('a', time())</code> formats it and returns <b>"am"</b> or <b>"pm"</b>. Both are <b>built-in functions</b>.</li><li>The PHP block sits <b>in the middle of HTML text</b>: "Good" and ", welcome to my website." are non-PHP code copied directly.</li></ul>At 3 pm the resulting HTML is <code>Good afternoon , welcome to my website.</code> (the space before the comma comes from the line break, which the browser shows as one space). The time used is the <b>server's</b> clock, since PHP runs on the server.

A <b>while loop</b> repeats its <b>loop body</b> as long as the <b>loop condition</b> is true. The condition is checked <b>before the first iteration</b> and <b>after every iteration</b>; if it is False at the start, the body runs <b>zero times</b>.<pre>$num = 17;
while ($num &lt; 100) {
  echo "{$num}&lt;br&gt;";
  $num += 17;
}</pre>Output: 17, 34, 51, 68, 85 on five lines (102 fails the condition). The body needs <b>{ }</b>. Remember to <b>update the variable</b> used in the condition, otherwise you get an <b>infinite loop</b>.<br>Loops are useful for generating repeated HTML, e.g. table rows of squares:<pre>$m = 10;
while ($m &lt; 21) {
  $square = $m ** 2;
  echo "&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;{$m}&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;{$square}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;";
  $m += 1;
}</pre>Changing the range later means editing one number, not rewriting static HTML.

ex.q:
What is the output?<pre>$s = 0;
for ($i = 1; $i &lt;= 10; $i += 3) {
  $s += $i;
}
echo $s;</pre>

cards.en:
The modulo operator tests divisibility: <code>$c % 3 == 0</code> means $c is a <b>multiple of 3</b>; <code>$num2 % $num1 == 0</code> means $num2 is <b>divisible by</b> $num1; <code>$n % 2 == 0</code> means $n is even.<br>FizzBuzz (1 to 100):<pre>for ($i = 1; $i &lt; 101; $i++) {
  if ($i % 3 == 0 and $i % 5 == 0) {
    echo "&lt;li&gt;Fizz Buzz&lt;/li&gt;";
  } elseif ($i % 3 == 0) {
    echo "&lt;li&gt;Fizz&lt;/li&gt;";
  } elseif ($i % 5 == 0) {
    echo "&lt;li&gt;Buzz&lt;/li&gt;";
  } else {
    echo "&lt;li&gt;{$i}&lt;/li&gt;";
  }
}</pre>Key point: the <b>"multiple of both 3 and 5" condition must come first</b>. If <code>$i % 3 == 0</code> were tested first, 15 would match it, print only Fizz, and the remaining branches would be skipped.

Two techniques used in the chapter exercises.<br><b>Swap</b> two variables with a temporary variable (writing <code>$num1 = $num2; $num2 = $num1;</code> loses the first value):<pre>if ($num1 &gt; $num2) {
  $temp = $num1;
  $num1 = $num2;
  $num2 = $temp;
}</pre>After this, $num1 ≤ $num2.<br><b>Linear search with a flag</b>: check elements one by one and stop once found.<pre>$found = false; $i = 0;
while ($found == false &amp;&amp; $i &lt; count($users)) {
  if ($username == $users[$i]) { $found = true; }
  $i++;
}
if ($found) { echo "Welcome {$username}!"; }
else { echo "The username does not exist."; }</pre>The condition <code>$i &lt; count($users)</code> stops the loop at the end of the array when the name is not there, so no non-existent index is read.

These are not in the textbook but your school may teach them.<br><b>switch</b> is an alternative to a long if-elseif chain comparing one value:<pre>switch ($role) {
  case "Manager":
    echo "Welcome Manager";
    break;
  case "Staff":
    echo "Welcome Staff";
    break;
  default:
    echo "Not Authorised!";
}</pre><b>Without break, execution falls through</b> into the next case. Matching uses loose comparison (==).<br><b>do-while</b> checks the condition <b>after</b> the body, so the body runs <b>at least once</b>:<pre>$i = 10;
do {
  echo $i;
  $i++;
} while ($i &lt; 5);   // outputs 10</pre><code>break</code> leaves a loop immediately; <code>continue</code> skips the rest of this iteration and goes to the next.

ex.q:
What is output?<pre>for ($i = 1; $i &lt;= 6; $i++) {
  if ($i == 3) { continue; }
  if ($i == 5) { break; }
  echo $i;
}</pre>

cards.en:
To show only matching items from parallel arrays, first collect the <b>indexes</b> that match, then loop through those indexes:<pre>$filtered = [];
for ($i = 0; $i &lt; count($items); $i++) {
  if (str_contains($items[$i], "tablet")) {
    $filtered[] = $i;
  }
}
foreach ($filtered as $v) {
  echo "&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;{$items[$v]}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;{$price[$v]}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;";
}</pre><b>str_contains($haystack, $needle)</b> returns true if the string contains the substring (PHP 8+; case-sensitive). Storing indexes (not values) lets us look up the matching element in <b>every</b> parallel array, e.g. both the item name and its price.

Describing one entity's <b>attributes</b> (e.g. a user's name, password, gender) is an <b>index-value mapping</b>. An <b>associative array</b> stores <b>index-value pairs</b> where the <b>index is a developer-defined string</b> (not consecutive numbers), and the value can be any data type. It is like a Python <b>dictionary</b> (index ≈ key).<pre>$user = [];                         // empty
$teacher["name"] = "Gloria";        // creates the array and its first pair
$teacher["subject"] = "Chemistry";  // add an element
$teacher["age"] = 24;
$teacher["subject"] = "Biology";    // same index again = edit
echo $teacher["name"];              // Gloria</pre>The index is a string, so <b>don't forget the double quotes</b>. (Supplement: <code>["name" =&gt; "Gloria", "age" =&gt; 24]</code> creates it in one go.)

To insert an array element in a double-quoted string, wrap it in braces:<pre>$flavours = ["choco", "vanilla", "caramel"];
echo "My favourite ice cream is {$flavours[1]}";   // vanilla
$user["name"] = "Thomas";
$user["sport"] = "swimming";
echo "The favourite sport of {$user["name"]} is {$user["sport"]}";</pre>For associative arrays, <b>complex parsing with { } is required</b> because the index itself has quotation marks.<br><b>Example 2.5</b>:<pre>$user["name"] = "Martin Lam";
$user["year_of_birth"] = 1993;
$age = date("Y") - $user["year_of_birth"];
echo "{$user["name"]} is {$age} years old.";</pre><code>date("Y")</code> returns the current 4-digit year as a string; as a numeric string it can be subtracted directly. In 2026 it outputs Martin Lam is 33 years old.

