function countFizzBuzz(n) {
  let c = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    if (i % 3 == 0 && i % 5 == 0) {
      c++;
    }
  }
  return c;
}

function findIndex(arr, target) {
  let found = false;
  let i = 0;
  while (!found && i < arr.length) {
    if (arr[i] == target) {
      found = true;
    } else {
      i++;
    }
  }
  return found ? i : -1;
}

cards.en:
An <b>HTML form</b> is defined by the <code>&lt;form&gt; ... &lt;/form&gt;</code> tag pair. The form itself has no visible appearance, but <b>all input elements must be placed within the form tag pair</b>; only fields inside the form are submitted together.<br><br>Two important attributes:<ul><li><b>action</b>: <b>specifies the location to send the form's data</b>, usually a PHP file on the server, e.g. <code>action="resultBMI.php"</code>.</li><li><b>method</b>: the HTTP request method used to send the data, <b>GET</b> or <b>POST</b>.</li></ul>If method is omitted the browser uses <b>GET</b> by default. If <code>action=""</code> (empty) the data is sent to <b>the same URL as the current page</b>.<pre>&lt;form action="process.php" method="POST"&gt;
  ... input fields ...
  &lt;input type="submit" name="submit"&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</pre>

Server-side validation in the book uses a <b>flag variable</b>:<pre>$valid = true;                     // 1. assume valid
if(empty($_POST["name"])){
  $valid = false;                  // presence check
}
if(empty($_POST["height"])){
  $valid = false;
}elseif($_POST["height"] &lt; 0 || $_POST["height"] &gt; 250){
  $valid = false;                  // range check
}
if($valid){
  // 3. process and output result
}else{
  echo "Invalid data entered!";
}</pre>Steps: <b>set the flag to true; any failed check sets it to false; finally decide by the flag</b>. Using <b>if … elseif</b> means the range check only runs if the presence check passed (avoids comparing a missing value). Each field is checked in its own if-block so all fields are examined. Summary for BMI: Name → presence; Height → presence + range (0–250); Weight → presence + range (0–250).

Because PHP generates HTML, a loop can output many form fields. Attendance example (Chapter Exercise):<pre>&lt;form action="absent.php" method="POST"&gt;
&lt;?php
for($i = 1; $i &lt;= $no_of_stud; $i++){
  echo "&lt;input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"present[]\" value=\"{$i}\"&gt; {$i}&lt;br&gt;";
}
?&gt;
&lt;input type="submit" name="submit"&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</pre>Inside a double-quoted PHP string, a literal <code>"</code> must be escaped as <b><code>\"</code></b>.<br><br>Processing side, finding absentees:<pre>$present = isset($_POST["present"]) ? $_POST["present"] : [];
$absent = [];
for($i = 1; $i &lt;= $no_of_stud; $i++){
  if(!in_array($i, $present)){ $absent[] = $i; }
}</pre><code>$absent[] = $i</code> <b>appends an element to the end of the array</b>. in_array's loose comparison lets the integer $i match the string values like "3".

Some validations check whether input matches one of many stored values, e.g. an activation code list, or whether an email is already registered.<pre>$found = false;
foreach($codes as $code){
  if($userinput == $code){ $found = true; }
}
if(!$found){ echo "Invalid activation code"; }</pre>This is a <b>linear search</b> with a flag. The built-in shortcut is <code>in_array($userinput, $codes)</code>.<br><br>Email registration (Chapter Exercise idea): first a <b>format check</b> (<code>strpos($email, "@")</code>), then loop over the registered emails; if one is equal, set <code>$valid = false</code> because <b>the email has already been registered</b>. In real systems the registered list is in the server's database, which is why this check can only be server-side.

