cards.en:
Inside a <b>double-quoted</b> string, insert an associative array element with <b>curly braces</b>: <code>"&lt;td&gt;{$row['code']}&lt;/td&gt;"</code>. A double-quoted PHP string <b>can span multiple lines</b>, so a whole &lt;tr&gt;...&lt;/tr&gt; can be echoed at once.<br><br>Structure of <b>list_restaurants.php</b>: connection code at the top (with die()), then HTML (heading, &lt;table&gt; and a header row &lt;th&gt;), then a PHP block that queries and loops, echoing one <b>&lt;tr&gt; per record</b>.<pre>&lt;table&gt;
 &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Code&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Name&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
 &lt;?php
 $result = mysqli_query($link, "SELECT * FROM restaurants");
 while ($row = mysqli_fetch_assoc($result)) {
   echo "&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;{$row['code']}&lt;/td&gt;
         &lt;td&gt;{$row['name']}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;";
 }
 ?&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;</pre>The user only receives the generated HTML.

A <b>successful</b> query may still return <b>no rows</b> (no record meets the condition). <b>mysqli_num_rows()</b> receives a query result object and <b>returns the number of rows</b>:<pre>$result = mysqli_query($link, "SELECT * FROM restaurants WHERE code = 2010");
if (mysqli_num_rows($result) == 0) {
  echo "This restaurant does not exist!";
} else {
  while ($row = mysqli_fetch_assoc($result)) { ... }
}</pre>Note: an empty result is still an object, so <code>if (!$result)</code> does <b>not</b> detect it.<br><br>For <b>COUNT / SUM / AVG / MAX / MIN</b>, give an <b>alias</b> and use it as the index:<pre>$result = mysqli_query($link, "SELECT COUNT(*) AS num_res FROM restaurants");
$row = mysqli_fetch_assoc($result);
echo "There are {$row["num_res"]} restaurants available.";</pre>

Inside the fetch loop you can use normal PHP: arithmetic, counters and if-statements.<br><b>Example 4.2 (topThree.php)</b>: list students who scored above 75 in all three subjects with rank and total.<pre>$query = "SELECT name, english, math, ict FROM student_marks
          WHERE english &gt; 75 AND math &gt; 75 AND ict &gt; 75
          ORDER BY english + math + ict DESC";
$result = mysqli_query($link, $query);
$i = 1;
while ($row = mysqli_fetch_assoc($result)) {
  $total = $row["english"] + $row["math"] + $row["ict"];
  echo "&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;{$i}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;{$row["name"]}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;{$total}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;";
  $i++;
}</pre><b>Book error</b>: the book writes <code>mysqli_query($query,$link)</code>; the correct order is ($link, $query). To rank by total, sort in SQL with ORDER BY.<br><br>Branching example: <code>if ($row["category"] == "Charging") { show sale_price, 'Yes' } else { show price, 'No' }</code>.

The City Search Engine form:<pre>&lt;form method="GET" action=""&gt;
  &lt;input type="text" name="city"&gt;&lt;br&gt;
  &lt;input type="submit" name="submit"&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</pre><ul><li><b>GET</b> is used because the client only <b>retrieves information</b>, <b>no change to the server's state</b>, and the URL <b>can be bookmarked, saved or shared</b>.</li><li><b>action=""</b> sends the form data to the <b>same URL as the current page</b>, so the results appear on the same page.</li><li>After submitting: <code>city_search.php?city=Hong+Kong&amp;submit=Search</code> (space encoded as +).</li></ul>In the script, check <code>if (isset($_GET["submit"]))</code> before searching, so nothing runs on the first visit. Use <b>POST</b> for forms that change data (e.g. adding a review with INSERT).

An SQL statement is just a string, so variables can change the filter or sort criteria at <b>runtime</b>:<pre>$c = "US";
$query = "SELECT name, country, population FROM city_info WHERE country = '{$c}'";</pre>⚠ For <b>text</b> fields put <b>single quotes</b> around the variable: <code>'{$c}'</code>. Forgetting them is a hard-to-find bug. <b>Numeric</b> fields do not need quotes: <code>price &lt;= {$max_price}</code>.<br><br><b>Dynamic WHERE with optional conditions</b> (Exercise 4):<pre>$sql = "SELECT * FROM headphones WHERE TRUE";
if (!empty($brand))     { $sql = $sql . " AND brand = '{$brand}'"; }
if (!empty($max_price)) { $sql = $sql . " AND price &lt;= {$max_price}"; }</pre><b>WHERE TRUE</b> lets every later condition start with AND. Join strings with <b>.</b> and keep a <b>space before AND</b>. (Book error: the exercise tests <code>!empty($price)</code> but the variable is <code>$max_price</code>.)

Forms that <b>change server state</b> (add a record) should use <b>POST</b>. Textbook Exercise 3 (rating.php):<pre>$game_id = mysqli_real_escape_string($conn, $_POST['game_id']);
$user_id = mysqli_real_escape_string($conn, $_POST['user_id']);
$rating  = mysqli_real_escape_string($conn, $_POST['rating']);
$query = "INSERT INTO review VALUES('$game_id','$user_id','$rating')";
if (mysqli_query($conn, $query)) {
  echo "Review added successfully.";
} else {
  echo "There is an error.";
}</pre>Points:<ul><li>INSERT returns <b>True</b> on success, False on failure, so it can be tested directly in if.</li><li>Values go in the same order as the table's columns (game_id, user_id, rating).</li><li>Variables can also be inserted without braces in double quotes: <code>'$game_id'</code>.</li><li>INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE are mainly Elective A content; know the return value for Elective B.</li></ul>

