cards.en:
In JavaScript a variable must be <b>declared with the keyword let</b> before use, and it should be given an initial value at the same time (<b>initialisation</b>).<pre>let price = 10;
let place = "Laputa";
price = 12;      // reassign: no let again</pre>After declaration, assign new values <b>without</b> writing let again; declaring the same name twice with let in the same scope is an error.<br><br><b>Enrichment: var, let, const</b><ul><li><b>var</b>: the old way; it causes confusing behaviour because of <b>variable scoping</b>.</li><li><b>let</b>: introduced in ES6 (ECMAScript 2015/16); more predictable, and recommended by MDN.</li><li><b>const</b>: a constant, <b>cannot be reassigned</b> after declaration.</li></ul>Unlike PHP, a JavaScript variable name does not need a $ sign.

