cards.en:
A <b>template literal</b> lets you insert variables (or expressions) directly into a string. Enclose the whole string in <b>backticks (`)</b> and write each variable as <b>${variable}</b>.<pre>let PUB = "Nicholas";
let msg = `${PUB} will do a great job`;
console.log(`Sum = ${2 + 3}`);   // Sum = 5</pre><b>Single or double quotes do not work</b>: <code>"Hi ${n}"</code> prints the characters ${n} literally.<br><br><b>Compare with PHP variable parsing</b>:<table><tr><th></th><th>PHP</th><th>JavaScript</th></tr><tr><td>Main string</td><td>double quotes " "</td><td>backticks ` `</td></tr><tr><td>Variable</td><td>{$name}</td><td>${name}</td></tr></table><pre>$msg = "Learn from {$name}";    // PHP
let msg = `Learn from ${name}`;  // JS</pre>

Example 5.1 practises the whole of 5.1 together: declaring with let, operator precedence, floor division and output.<pre>let x = 20;
let y = 4;
let z = (x / y) ** 2;            // 25
console.log(Math.floor(z / 7));   // 3
console.log("The values of x and y are " + x + " and " + y);
console.log(`The values of x and y are ${x} and ${y}`);</pre>Exam tips: (1) evaluate brackets, then **, then * / %, then + -; (2) floor division needs <b>Math.floor()</b>; (3) when concatenating, check the <b>spaces</b> in the output exactly; (4) both concatenation and template literal give the same text.

A <b>while loop</b> executes a set of statements repeatedly <b>as long as the loop condition is true</b>. The condition is checked <b>before</b> each iteration, so the body may run zero times.<pre>let num = 17;
while (num &lt; 100) {
  console.log(num);
  num += 17;
}
// 17 34 51 68 85</pre>Something inside the loop must change the condition, otherwise you get an <b>infinite loop</b> and the browser tab freezes. Use a while loop when the number of repetitions is <b>not known in advance</b> (e.g. keep doubling until a value exceeds a limit).

ex.q:
