cards.en:
The browser represents the page as the <b>DOM (Document Object Model)</b>, a tree of objects that JavaScript can access through the <b>DOM API</b>. The object <b>document</b> stands for the whole page.<br><b>document.getElementById("id")</b> returns the element whose id matches; if none matches it returns <b>null</b>.<pre>let p = document.getElementById("name");
p.innerText = "Linda";
// or in one line
document.getElementById("name").innerText = "Linda";</pre>Watch the spelling: get<b>E</b>lement<b>B</b>y<b>I</b>d with capital E, B, I and lower-case d; the id is a <b>string in quotes</b>.

If the statement that changes the text is written directly in &lt;script&gt;, it runs <b>immediately when the page loads</b> (sequentially), not when the user clicks. So we group the code in a <b>function</b>, which only runs when it is <b>invoked (called)</b>.<pre>function showName() {
  document.getElementById("name").innerText = "Linda";
}
showName();   // invoke</pre>Syntax: keyword <b>function</b>, the function name, round brackets ( ), and the body in { }. Defining a function does not run it. Functions also let you reuse code and keep the page's functionality in &lt;head&gt;, separate from the layout.

A function can receive <b>parameters</b> and send back a value with <b>return</b>:<pre>function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}
let s = add(3, 4);   // 7</pre>The values passed when calling (3 and 4) are the <b>arguments</b>; a and b are the <b>parameters</b>. return ends the function immediately and gives the value to the caller. A function without return gives <b>undefined</b>. Variables declared with let inside a function are <b>local</b>: they cannot be used outside it. Parameters make one function serve many cases, e.g. update(k) for both +1 and -1 buttons.

Section 5.4 designs a <b>counter</b>: a &lt;p id="counter"&gt;0&lt;/p&gt; and buttons that add or subtract. The handler follows three steps every time:<ol><li><b>Retrieve</b> the current value: <code>let x = document.getElementById("counter").innerText;</code></li><li><b>Process</b>: <code>x = Number(x) + 1;</code></li><li><b>Update</b> the page: <code>document.getElementById("counter").innerText = x;</code></li></ol><pre>function add() {
  let x = document.getElementById("counter").innerText;
  x = Number(x) + 1;
  document.getElementById("counter").innerText = x;
}</pre>&lt;button onclick="add()"&gt;Add&lt;/button&gt;. The page stores its state in the element itself, and nothing is sent to the server.

Instead of writing add() and subtract() separately, write <b>one function with a parameter</b> and pass a different <b>argument</b> in each onclick:<pre>function update(k) {
  let x = document.getElementById("counter").innerText;
  x = Number(x) + k;
  document.getElementById("counter").innerText = x;
}</pre><pre>&lt;button onclick="update(1)"&gt;Add&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;button onclick="update(-1)"&gt;Subtract&lt;/button&gt;</pre>Benefits: less repeated code; easier to maintain (a change is made once). The same idea lets one function update several counters, e.g. update("c2", 1) where the first argument is the <b>id</b> of the counter.

For <b>&lt;input&gt;</b> elements, the text the user typed is in the <b>value</b> property, not innerText:<pre>let g = document.getElementById("guess").value;
let n = Number(document.getElementById("input").value);
document.getElementById("input").value = "";   // clear the box</pre>.value is also a <b>String</b>, even for type="number", so use Number() before calculating. Assigning "" resets the input field after the user clicks a button. Typical flow (book exercises): read input → <b>validate</b> (e.g. 0 to 100) → process (push into array) → update the page.

